65,3,1,talk,Mio,,"Option A is the most predictable for our budget, but after seeing those bank statements, I'm worried a big upfront cost might be too much for some people to handle at once!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"Option C seems risky. If we attach charges to each event, we might reinforce that hesitation I mentioned, making members weigh the cost every time they consider joining us.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,1,talk,Takumi,,Option A is a joke. It's just a way to let the ghost members get a free ride while the regulars do all the work. We need something that actually reflects who is using the club!,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Option C is out of the question. As I mentioned, chasing small payments for every event is an administrative nightmare that will lead to treasurer burnout. It is simply not sustainable.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"Option B sounds interesting! It lets people choose how much they want to commit to, which might help those taster guests who are scared of a huge annual fee.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,2,talk,Mio,,"@Kenji, I agree! Option B could be a great middle ground. It gives us a clearer idea of what's coming in than Option C, but it's not as scary as one giant bill like Option A.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Takumi, I understand your frustration, but Option B might be a better way to acknowledge different levels of commitment without making every single evening feel like a transaction. It preserves the sense of belonging.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Jonathan, as long as the tiers actually reflect the usage and the work being done, I can live with it. It's still better than Option A, where the regulars basically pay for the ghosts.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"Option B is far more manageable than C. While it requires some initial setup, it avoids the constant cycle of chasing small sums. It protects the treasurer's time while offering more flexibility than A.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Toshio, that's a relief! If it's easier for you, then it's a win. I just hope the tiers aren't too strict, so people can still feel like they're part of the group regardless of which one they pick!","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,3,talk,Mio,,"@Takumi, if we set the tiers carefully, we can make sure the regulars feel valued without making it too hard for others to join. It feels much safer than guessing a single flat fee!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Mio, I agree. If we can create a structure where the cost is settled at the start, we remove the friction of deciding whether to attend each week. It allows the club to remain a sanctuary of peace.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,3,talk,Takumi,,"I'm still skeptical, but if Option B means the people who actually show up aren't subsidizing the ones who don't, I'm in. Let's just make sure the tiers are actually meaningful.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,3,talk,Toshio,,Option B is the only logical compromise. It satisfies the need for flexibility and fairness without sacrificing the treasurer's sanity. It is a sustainable system that ensures the role remains viable for the next person.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"I'm really happy we're all agreeing! As long as the tiers are welcoming and don't make people feel judged for how much they can give or attend, I think it's the best way to go!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
80,3,4,talk,Mio,,"I'm so glad we found a solution that works for everyone! Option B is definitely the way to go—it's fair, welcoming, and most importantly, it gives us a predictable budget. I vote for Option B!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
81,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,I believe Option B is the most thoughtful choice. It removes the transactional nature of each visit while remaining accessible to all. I vote for Option B.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
82,3,4,talk,Takumi,,I'm voting for Option B. It's the only way to stop the regulars from getting ripped off while keeping the club running. B it is!,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
83,3,4,talk,Toshio,,Option B is the most sustainable choice for the administration and the most reasonable compromise for the members. I vote for Option B.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
84,3,4,talk,Kenji,,I'm so glad we could all agree! Option B is the perfect way to make sure new friends feel welcome while keeping things easy for everyone. I vote for Option B!,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
